course: dictionary
lesson: 3404
title: Expletive
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: An expletive is a swear word or a rude exclamation, said when someone is angry, hurt or surprised. Writers often leave the swear word out and show the gap instead. «Expletive deleted» means a swear word was said here and has been left out of the written record.
explain: Writers use cover words so they do not have to print the real word: «strong language», «colourful language», «some choice words» and «expletive deleted». Each one tells you that rude words were used, without showing them. Context clues like these help you understand the meaning even when the word is missing.
explain: Grammar gives expletive a second meaning. It is a dummy word, such as «it» or «there», that fills a place in a sentence without meaning anything. In «There is a cat» and «It is raining», the words «there» and «it» are expletives in this sense.
fact: An expletive is a swear word or a rude exclamation.
fact: «Expletive deleted» means a swear word was said here and has been left out.
fact: «A string of expletives» means several swear words one after another.
fact: In grammar, an expletive is a dummy word, such as «it» or «there», that fills a place in a sentence.
fact: Expletives appear in stories, news and formal writing, and we talk about them when we discuss polite language.
exercise: «So they use cover words, like «expletive deleted».» What does «expletive deleted» tell you? | *A swear word was said here and has been left out | A big explosion happened here | The word was spelled wrong | Someone won a prize
exercise: In the sentence «It is raining», what job does «it» do? | A verb meaning to fall | A name for the weather | A swear word said when angry | *A dummy word that fills the place in the sentence without meaning anything
exercise: Ella hears «a string of expletives» from the angry man. What does that mean? | Lots of quiet whispers | *Several swear words said one after another | A list of rude names for towns | A long piece of string
test: In «There is a cat», what is «there» doing? | Naming the cat | Showing the cat is angry | Pointing to a cat in a field | *Filling the place in the sentence without a real meaning
test: Why might a news report print «expletive deleted» instead of the swear word? | *Because the swear word was left out of the written record | Because it is the title of a famous book | Because the reporter forgot the word | Because the word is very long
exam: What are the two meanings of expletive? | A hard test and an easy game | *A swear word or rude exclamation, and a dummy word like «it» or «there» in grammar | A rude animal and a kind bird | A loud noise and a quiet whisper
